    "result_text": "The build passes green. There's exactly one test class in the repo (`AcceptanceTest`), and it now passes along with the full compile.\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed the `customerCode` format from `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` to `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` with a per-city sequence.\n\n**`AssignOwnerCustomerCode.java`** \u2014 the single place that builds the code:\n- `CITY3` = upper-cased first three letters of the city, `LAST3` = first three of the last name (shared via one `prefix(...)` helper, since both do the identical thing).\n- `NNNN` = per-city 4-digit sequence = one more than the number of owners already in that city, computed by filtering `findAll()` on the owner's city (the entity isn't saved yet, so the new owner isn't counted). This replaced the old global `findAll().size() + 1`.\n- Updated the class Javadoc to match.\n\n**Membership number** \u2014 no code change needed. `AssignOwnerMembershipNumber` builds `<customerCode>-M<YY>` by reading `owner.getCustomerCode()` at runtime, so it automatically rebuilds on the new format (e.g. `PAR-SMI-0007-M26`). It was the only value derived from `customerCode` (household ID and namesake count are unrelated).\n\n**`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `customerCode` and `membershipNumber` schema descriptions and examples to reflect the three-segment format, keeping the docs consistent with behavior.\n\nThe acceptance test's regex `[A-Z]{3}-[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}` now matches, and the per-city sequence logic satisfies the \"one more than the owners already in that city\" requirement.",
